      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Writing a rejection email can be a delicate task, but I'm here to help. You want to be kind, yet clear, so the candidate doesn't get their hopes up. It's a delicate balance, like trying to navigate a treacherous mountain pass without getting lost in the fog. You need to be firm, yet gentle, like a goblin guide leading a group through the misty peaks.\n\nHere's a sample email that might help you get started:\n\n\"Dear [Candidate Name],\n\nI wanted to personally reach out to you regarding the status of your application for the [Position] role at [Company]. After careful consideration, I regret to inform you that we will not be moving forward with your application at this time.\n\nPlease know that this decision was not made lightly, and we were impressed by your skills and experience during the interview process. However, we have decided to go with another candidate whose qualifications better align with the needs of our team at this time.\n\nI want to thank you for taking the time to interview with us and for your interest in joining our team. We appreciate the enthusiasm and energy you brought to the conversation.\n\nBest regards,\n[Your Name]\"\n\nRemember to keep the tone polite and professional, but also clear and",
